From 2840ddb6c1ef098f3674872a38282fd330be8076 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:39:41 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] linux: don't fail epoll_ctl(2) for events a file cannot
 report

Linux accepts any event mask for a pollable file and simply never
reports events the file cannot produce. epoll_ctl translates EPOLLIN and
EPOLLOUT into EVFILT_READ and EVFILT_WRITE kevents and registered both in
one kevent call, so a file whose kqfilter only supports EVFILT_READ
(inotify, timerfd, kqueue) failed the whole call with EINVAL - after the
read filter had already been added, so a retry got EEXIST.

Go's netpoller registers every non-blocking descriptor for
EPOLLIN|EPOLLOUT|EPOLLRDHUP|EPOLLET. With the failure, Go treated inotify
descriptors as not pollable and reads on them returned EAGAIN, which
broke fsnotify (e.g. certificate reloading in Kubernetes components).

Register each filter separately, skip filters the file rejects with
EINVAL as long as one was registered, and undo a partial registration
on any other error.
---
 sys/compat/linux/linux_event.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/compat/linux/linux_event.c b/sys/compat/linux/linux_event.c
index add7d2b4e..d1bd88747 100644
--- a/sys/compat/linux/linux_event.c
+++ b/sys/compat/linux/linux_event.c
@@ -291,7 +291,7 @@ linux_epoll_ctl(struct thread *td, struct linux_epoll_ctl_args *args)
 	struct epoll_event le;
 	cap_rights_t rights;
 	int nchanges = 0;
-	int error;
+	int error, i, registered;
 
 	if (args->op != LINUX_EPOLL_CTL_DEL) {
 		error = copyin(args->event, &le, sizeof(le));
@@ -372,7 +372,28 @@ linux_epoll_ctl(struct thread *td, struct linux_epoll_ctl_args *args)
 		goto leave0;
 	}
 
-	error = kern_kevent_fp(td, epfp, nchanges, 0, &k_ops, NULL);
+	/*
+	 * Register each filter on its own.  Linux accepts any event mask for a
+	 * pollable file and never reports events the file cannot produce,
+	 * e.g. EPOLLOUT on an inotify or timerfd descriptor.  Their FreeBSD
+	 * kqfilters reject EVFILT_WRITE with EINVAL, which used to fail the
+	 * whole call.  Go's netpoller registers every descriptor for
+	 * EPOLLIN|EPOLLOUT, so such files were not pollable and reads on them
+	 * returned EAGAIN (e.g. fsnotify).  Skip filters the file does not
+	 * support as long as one of them was registered.
+	 */
+	registered = 0;
+	for (i = 0; i < nchanges; i++) {
+		error = kern_kevent_fp(td, epfp, 1, 0, &k_ops, NULL);
+		if (error == 0)
+			registered++;
+		else if (error != EINVAL)
+			break;
+	}
+	if (error == EINVAL && registered > 0)
+		error = 0;
+	else if (error != 0 && registered > 0)
+		(void)epoll_delete_all_events(td, epfp, args->fd);
 
 leave0:
 	fdrop(fp, td);
